---
Part II, Chapter 6: The multi-file AST
Package: packages/loom-ast/src/ast/LoomCorpusAst.ts
Language: TypeScript
---

# The multi-file AST

`LoomCorpusAst` is the multi-file AST — pure data, no pipeline (that is `LoomCorpusAstBuilder`). Every loaded `.loom` is a `LoomModule` keyed by its path, holding its source, parsed `doc`, its composed `product`, and import edges. The corpus is just a map of those modules. The `product` is what the product pass builds for each module; a cross-file reference stays an edge there, resolved later at projection by `fromProduct`.

## The module and the corpus

A `Location` names a loom, and it is a module's identity. The source that keeps the loom makes it, as the chapter "Where a workspace is kept" describes, and the corpus compares locations and keys its maps by them. On the file system a location is the loom's resolved path. A `LoomModule` carries one file's `product` beside its `text` and parsed `doc`. The `product` is the module's composition graph, built straight from the parse, so every loaded module carries one. A `LoomCorpusAst` is the map of modules by path.

=>

export type Location = string

export const LoomModuleSchema = Schema.Struct({
  path: Schema.String,
  text: Schema.String,
  doc: LoomDocumentSchema,
  product: ProductSchema,
})
export type LoomModule = typeof LoomModuleSchema.Type

export const LoomCorpusAstSchema = Schema.Struct({
  modules: Schema.ReadonlyMap(Schema.String, LoomModuleSchema),
})
export type LoomCorpusAst = typeof LoomCorpusAstSchema.Type

## The module map

A `Modules` map keys each loom in a corpus by its path. The corpus is one map; the graph over it is the anchor graph, where a section in one loom composes a section in another. The forward reach a run and a tangle scope to, and the reverse reach an edit reprojects, both walk that graph, so both live beside it.

=>

type Modules = ReadonlyMap<Location, LoomModule>

## Resolving names

Names resolve here: the lookups that turn an `::[Name]` anchor into the section it reaches. A `SectionRef` locates that section by the module it lives in and the section itself.

A name resolves nearest first, outward from the anchor. A bare `::[Name]` looks first among the sections of its own loom. When none of them carries the name, it looks among the chapters of the book, and a chapter it finds stands for the chapter's opening section. The book is the whole corpus. The rule holds wherever the anchor stands, in a section's code as in its prose.

An author names a section of another chapter with a path. A path lists names from the outside in, separated by commas, and each name after the first lies inside the one before it. `::[The multi-file AST, Resolving names]` names a section of this chapter from anywhere in the book, and `::[The shape of a loom, The multi-file AST, Resolving names]` names the same section through its part. A comma splits a name into a path only when the whole name matches nothing, so a title that holds a comma, such as `Once, as the site is built`, still resolves whole.

A name and a path of names are the only ways an anchor reaches a section. Loom reads no loom's path in an anchor, and the product pass faults an anchor that still writes one in parentheses after its name.

`titlesIn` indexes one loom's titled sections by their raw title. The first of any duplicate within the loom wins. An anchor that names a duplicated title gets no such tie-break, because the product pass reports it as ambiguous. `titleIndexes` builds that index for every loom, once.

=>

export type SectionRef = { readonly module: Location; readonly section: LoomSection }

const titlesIn = (module: LoomModule): ReadonlyMap<string, SectionRef> =>
  pipe(
    module.doc.sections,
    Array.map((section) =>
      Option.map(
        Option.fromNullishOr(section.heading.title),
        (title) => [title.source, { module: module.path, section }] as const,
      ),
    ),
    Array.getSomes,
    Array.reduce(new Map<string, SectionRef>(), (index, [title, ref]) =>
      index.has(title) ? index : new Map(index).set(title, ref),
    ),
  )

export type TitleIndexes = ReadonlyMap<Location, ReadonlyMap<string, SectionRef>>

export const titleIndexes = (modules: Modules): TitleIndexes =>
  new Map(Array.fromIterable(modules.values()).map((m) => [m.path, titlesIn(m)] as const))

~

The book finds a chapter by the title the chapter declares, which `chapterTitleToken` reads here and in the navigation below. A `Chapter` pairs that title with the loom that declares it, its own number, and the number and the name of the part it belongs to. A chapter states its part in one of three ways. A part's opening chapter announces the part's number and name, as `Part IV: The product` does. Every other chapter of a numbered part gives only the number, as `Part IV, Chapter 2` does. A chapter outside the numbered parts gives the part's name alone, as `Part: Work in progress` does.

`partNamesIn` maps each part number to the name its opening chapter announces. `partOf` reads the part a chapter belongs to as a `PartRef`: the number the chapter gives, and the name it gives or its number maps to. `samePart` tells whether two parts are one, by number when both give one and by name otherwise. `chaptersIn` lists every chapter of the book, so two chapters that share a title both stay in reach. `openingSectionOf` gives a loom's first titled section, the section that stands for its chapter.

=>

type PartRef = {
  readonly number: Option.Option<string>
  readonly name: Option.Option<string>
}

type Chapter = {
  readonly title: string
  readonly number: Option.Option<string>
  readonly part: PartRef
  readonly module: LoomModule
}

const partNamesIn = (modules: Modules): ReadonlyMap<string, string> =>
  new Map(
    Array.getSomes(
      Array.map(Array.fromIterable(modules.values()), (module) =>
        pipe(
          Option.fromNullishOr(module.doc.frontmatter),
          Option.flatMap((fm) =>
            Option.all([Option.fromNullishOr(fm.part), Option.fromNullishOr(fm.partName)]),
          ),
          Option.map(([part, partName]) => [part.value, partName.value] as const),
        ),
      ),
    ),
  )

const partOf = (names: ReadonlyMap<string, string>, module: LoomModule): PartRef => {
  const frontmatter = Option.fromNullishOr(module.doc.frontmatter)
  const number = pipe(
    frontmatter,
    Option.flatMapNullishOr((fm) => fm.part),
    Option.map((part) => part.value),
  )
  return {
    number,
    name: Option.orElse(
      pipe(
        frontmatter,
        Option.flatMapNullishOr((fm) => fm.partName),
        Option.map((partName) => partName.value),
      ),
      () => Option.flatMap(number, (n) => Option.fromNullishOr(names.get(n))),
    ),
  }
}

const samePart = (part: PartRef, other: PartRef): boolean =>
  Option.match(Option.all([part.number, other.number]), {
    onSome: ([number, otherNumber]) => number === otherNumber,
    onNone: () => Option.exists(part.name, (name) => Option.contains(other.name, name)),
  })

const chaptersIn = (modules: Modules): ReadonlyArray<Chapter> => {
  const names = partNamesIn(modules)
  return Array.getSomes(
    Array.map(Array.fromIterable(modules.values()), (module) =>
      Option.map(chapterTitleToken(module), (title) => ({
        title: title.source,
        number: Option.map(
          Option.fromNullishOr(module.doc.frontmatter?.chapter),
          (chapter) => chapter.value,
        ),
        part: partOf(names, module),
        module,
      })),
    ),
  )
}

const openingSectionOf = (module: LoomModule): Option.Option<LoomSection> =>
  Array.findFirst(module.doc.sections, (section) => section.heading.title !== undefined)

~

A `Book` gathers what a lookup reads: each loom's titles, every chapter, and each loom's chapter boxes, which the section "Boxes that point to chapters" describes. `bookOf` builds it from the modules once, for a caller that resolves many names over the same corpus.

=>

export type Book = {
  readonly titles: TitleIndexes
  readonly chapters: ReadonlyArray<Chapter>
  readonly boxes: ReadonlyMap<Location, ReadonlyArray<BoxRef>>
}

export const bookOf = (modules: Modules): Book => ({
  titles: titleIndexes(modules),
  chapters: chaptersIn(modules),
  boxes: new Map(
    Array.map(Array.fromIterable(modules.values()), (module) => [
      module.path,
      chapterBoxesOf(module),
    ]),
  ),
})

~

A lookup walks the book from the outside in, and records where each name led. A `Place` is one stop on the way: a part, a chapter, a section, or a box that points to a chapter nobody has written yet, which the section "Boxes that point to chapters" describes. A `Segment` is one name of a path, with its offsets inside the anchor's name, so the editor can rewrite that one name in a rename. A `Step` pairs a segment with the place it named, and records whether the segment named it by number. A `Trail` lists the steps of one resolution, from the outermost name to the place the anchor reaches, and a name that resolves whole leaves a trail of one step.

=>

export type Place = Data.TaggedEnum<{
  Part: { readonly part: PartRef }
  Chapter: { readonly chapter: Chapter }
  Section: { readonly ref: SectionRef }
  Box: { readonly ref: BoxRef }
}>

const Place = Data.taggedEnum<Place>()

export type Segment = { readonly text: string; readonly start: number; readonly end: number }

export type Step = { readonly segment: Segment; readonly place: Place; readonly numbered: boolean }

export type Trail = Array.NonEmptyReadonlyArray<Step>

~

A name in a path is a title or a number. `Part II` names a part by its number and `Chapter 6` a chapter by its number, the way a loom's identity line names them, and any other name is a title. `numberIn` reads the number out of a name that opens with a keyword, `Part` or `Chapter`.

`stepsNamed` finds the places a segment can mean inside a scope, and records for each whether the segment named it by number. At the top of the book, a segment names a part or a chapter. Inside a part, it names a chapter of that part. Inside a chapter, it names one of the chapter's sections by its title, since a section has no number, or one of the chapter's chapter boxes by its name. Inside a section or a box, it finds nothing, so the walk ends there. `partsWhere` finds a part by a test on its `PartRef` and keeps the reference of the first chapter in it, which carries the part's number and its name both. `chaptersWithin` finds the chapters of a scope that pass a test.

`walk` follows a path's segments through `stepsNamed`, one scope deeper for each. A trail is `finished` when it ends at a chapter or a section. A part stands for no section, so a trail that ends at a part resolves nothing, and a bare name never finds a part.

=>

const numberIn = (keyword: string, name: string): Option.Option<string> =>
  Option.fromNullishOr(new RegExp(`^${keyword}\\s+(\\S+)$`).exec(name)?.[1])

const chaptersWithin = (
  book: Book,
  scope: Option.Option<PartRef>,
  test: (chapter: Chapter) => boolean,
): ReadonlyArray<Chapter> =>
  Array.filter(
    book.chapters,
    (chapter) =>
      test(chapter) &&
      Option.match(scope, {
        onNone: () => true,
        onSome: (part) => samePart(chapter.part, part),
      }),
  )

const partsWhere = (book: Book, test: (part: PartRef) => boolean): ReadonlyArray<PartRef> =>
  Option.toArray(
    Option.map(
      Array.findFirst(book.chapters, (chapter) => test(chapter.part)),
      (chapter) => chapter.part,
    ),
  )

const sectionTitled = (book: Book, module: Location, title: string): Option.Option<SectionRef> =>
  Option.fromNullishOr(book.titles.get(module)?.get(title))

const stepsNamed = (
  book: Book,
  scope: Option.Option<Place>,
  segment: Segment,
): ReadonlyArray<Step> => {
  const step =
    (numbered: boolean) =>
    (place: Place): Step => ({ segment, place, numbered })
  const chapterSteps = (part: Option.Option<PartRef>): ReadonlyArray<Step> => [
    ...Array.map(
      chaptersWithin(book, part, (chapter) => chapter.title === segment.text),
      (chapter) => step(false)(Place.Chapter({ chapter })),
    ),
    ...Option.match(numberIn('Chapter', segment.text), {
      onNone: (): ReadonlyArray<Step> => [],
      onSome: (number) =>
        Array.map(
          chaptersWithin(book, part, (chapter) => Option.contains(chapter.number, number)),
          (chapter) => step(true)(Place.Chapter({ chapter })),
        ),
    }),
  ]
  return Option.match(scope, {
    onNone: () => [
      ...Array.map(
        partsWhere(book, (part) => Option.contains(part.name, segment.text)),
        (part) => step(false)(Place.Part({ part })),
      ),
      ...Option.match(numberIn('Part', segment.text), {
        onNone: (): ReadonlyArray<Step> => [],
        onSome: (number) =>
          Array.map(
            partsWhere(book, (part) => Option.contains(part.number, number)),
            (part) => step(true)(Place.Part({ part })),
          ),
      }),
      ...chapterSteps(Option.none()),
    ],
    onSome: (place) =>
      Match.value(place).pipe(
        Match.tag('Part', ({ part }) => chapterSteps(Option.some(part))),
        Match.tag('Chapter', ({ chapter }) =>
          Option.toArray(
            Option.orElse(
              Option.map(sectionTitled(book, chapter.module.path, segment.text), (ref) =>
                step(false)(Place.Section({ ref })),
              ),
              () =>
                Option.map(boxNamed(book, chapter.module.path, segment.text), (ref) =>
                  step(false)(boxPlace(book, ref)),
                ),
            ),
          ),
        ),
        Match.tag('Section', (): ReadonlyArray<Step> => []),
        Match.tag('Box', (): ReadonlyArray<Step> => []),
        Match.exhaustive,
      ),
  })
}

const finished = (trail: ReadonlyArray<Step>): ReadonlyArray<Trail> =>
  Array.isReadonlyArrayNonEmpty(trail) && Array.lastNonEmpty(trail).place._tag !== 'Part'
    ? [trail]
    : []

const walk = (
  book: Book,
  trail: ReadonlyArray<Step>,
  segments: ReadonlyArray<Segment>,
): ReadonlyArray<Trail> =>
  Array.matchLeft(segments, {
    onEmpty: () => finished(trail),
    onNonEmpty: (segment, rest) =>
      Array.flatMap(
        stepsNamed(
          book,
          Option.map(Array.last(trail), (step) => step.place),
          segment,
        ),
        (step) => walk(book, Array.append(trail, step), rest),
      ),
  })

~

`trailsOf` puts the four steps in their order. It looks among the sections of the anchor's own loom, then among its chapter boxes, then among the chapters, then along the name split into a path, and `nearest` keeps the first step that finds anything. `whole` makes a name into one segment that spans all of it, and `segmentsOf` splits a name at its commas, trimming the spaces around each. Every trail `trailsOf` returns lies at the nearest scope where the name matches at all. More than one trail is an ambiguity, which the faults report and the navigation declines to guess through.

`anchorTrails` resolves an anchor token by its name, through `trailsOf`. `sectionOf` reads the section a trail reaches, which is either the section itself or a chapter's opening section. A box whose chapter is not written yet reaches no section. `resolveName` resolves a name to the one section it reaches, and to nothing when no trail or more than one is found. `resolveAnchor` does the same for an anchor token.

=>

const whole = (name: string): Segment => ({ text: name, start: 0, end: name.length })

const segmentsOf = (name: string): ReadonlyArray<Segment> =>
  Array.map(Array.fromIterable(name.matchAll(/[^,]+/g)), (match) => {
    const text = match[0].trim()
    const start = match.index + match[0].length - match[0].trimStart().length
    return { text, start, end: start + text.length }
  })

const sectionTrail = (book: Book, module: Location, name: string): ReadonlyArray<Trail> =>
  Option.toArray(
    Option.map(sectionTitled(book, module, name), (ref): Trail => [
      { segment: whole(name), place: Place.Section({ ref }), numbered: false },
    ]),
  )

const nothingFound: ReadonlyArray<Trail> = []

const nearest = (lookups: ReadonlyArray<() => ReadonlyArray<Trail>>): ReadonlyArray<Trail> =>
  Array.reduce(lookups, nothingFound, (found, lookup) => (found.length > 0 ? found : lookup()))

export const trailsOf = (book: Book, from: Location, name: string): ReadonlyArray<Trail> =>
  nearest([
    () => sectionTrail(book, from, name),
    () => boxTrail(book, from, name),
    () => walk(book, [], [whole(name)]),
    () => walk(book, [], segmentsOf(name)),
  ])

export const anchorTrails = (
  book: Book,
  from: Location,
  anchor: WarpAnchorToken,
): ReadonlyArray<Trail> => trailsOf(book, from, anchor.name.value)

export const sectionOf = (trail: Trail): Option.Option<SectionRef> =>
  Match.value(Array.lastNonEmpty(trail).place).pipe(
    Match.tag('Section', ({ ref }) => Option.some(ref)),
    Match.tag('Chapter', ({ chapter }) =>
      Option.map(openingSectionOf(chapter.module), (section) => ({
        module: chapter.module.path,
        section,
      })),
    ),
    Match.tag('Part', () => Option.none<SectionRef>()),
    Match.tag('Box', () => Option.none<SectionRef>()),
    Match.exhaustive,
  )

const soleTrail = (trails: ReadonlyArray<Trail>): Option.Option<Trail> =>
  trails.length === 1 ? Array.head(trails) : Option.none()

export const resolveName = (book: Book, from: Location, name: string): Option.Option<SectionRef> =>
  Option.flatMap(soleTrail(trailsOf(book, from, name)), sectionOf)

export const resolveAnchor = (
  book: Book,
  from: Location,
  anchor: WarpAnchorToken,
): Option.Option<SectionRef> =>
  Option.flatMap(soleTrail(anchorTrails(book, from, anchor)), sectionOf)

~

An ambiguous name reaches several places, and a longer path can name each of them alone. `pathsTo` gives, for each trail of an ambiguous name, the shortest path that leads from the anchor's loom to that trail's place and to nothing else. The faults offer these paths as fixes.

`namesFor` lists the paths that can name a place, shortest first. A path names a chapter by its title, or by its part and its title. A path names a section by its chapter and its title, or by the chapter's part, the chapter, and the title, and it names a box the same way, by the box's name. `chapterOf` finds the chapter a section's loom declares, and `throughPart` adds the longer path when the chapter belongs to a part. `samePlace` compares two places, and `leadsTo` resolves a path through `trailsOf` and accepts it when its one trail ends at the place. A place that no path names alone gets none, such as one of two sections that share a title in one loom.

=>

const chapterOf = (book: Book, module: Location): Option.Option<Chapter> =>
  Array.findFirst(book.chapters, (chapter) => chapter.module.path === module)

const throughPart = (
  chapter: Chapter,
  names: ReadonlyArray<string>,
): ReadonlyArray<ReadonlyArray<string>> => [
  names,
  ...Option.toArray(Option.map(chapter.part.name, (part) => [part, ...names])),
]

const namesFor = (book: Book, place: Place): ReadonlyArray<ReadonlyArray<string>> =>
  Match.value(place).pipe(
    Match.tag('Chapter', ({ chapter }) => throughPart(chapter, [chapter.title])),
    Match.tag('Section', ({ ref }) =>
      Option.match(
        Option.all([chapterOf(book, ref.module), Option.fromNullishOr(ref.section.heading.title)]),
        {
          onNone: (): ReadonlyArray<ReadonlyArray<string>> => [],
          onSome: ([chapter, title]) => throughPart(chapter, [chapter.title, title.source]),
        },
      ),
    ),
    Match.tag('Box', ({ ref }) =>
      Option.match(chapterOf(book, ref.module), {
        onNone: (): ReadonlyArray<ReadonlyArray<string>> => [],
        onSome: (chapter) => throughPart(chapter, [chapter.title, ref.name.value]),
      }),
    ),
    Match.tag('Part', (): ReadonlyArray<ReadonlyArray<string>> => []),
    Match.exhaustive,
  )

const samePlace = (place: Place, other: Place): boolean =>
  Match.value(place).pipe(
    Match.tag('Part', ({ part }) => other._tag === 'Part' && samePart(part, other.part)),
    Match.tag(
      'Chapter',
      ({ chapter }) =>
        other._tag === 'Chapter' && other.chapter.module.path === chapter.module.path,
    ),
    Match.tag('Section', ({ ref }) => namesSection(ref)(other)),
    Match.tag('Box', ({ ref }) => namesBox(ref)(other)),
    Match.exhaustive,
  )

const leadsTo =
  (book: Book, from: Location, place: Place) =>
  (path: string): boolean =>
    Option.exists(soleTrail(trailsOf(book, from, path)), (trail) =>
      samePlace(Array.lastNonEmpty(trail).place, place),
    )

export const pathsTo = (
  book: Book,
  from: Location,
  trails: ReadonlyArray<Trail>,
): ReadonlyArray<string> =>
  Array.getSomes(
    Array.map(trails, (trail) => {
      const place = Array.lastNonEmpty(trail).place
      return Array.findFirst(
        Array.map(namesFor(book, place), (names) => Array.join(names, ', ')),
        leadsTo(book, from, place),
      )
    }),
  )

~

`anchorsOf` gathers every anchor a section carries — across its preamble and its body, code and prose alike — since the reachability walk and the navigation read the same set.

=>

const anchorsOf = (section: LoomSection): ReadonlyArray<WarpAnchorToken> =>
  pipe(
    [...section.preamble, ...section.code],
    Array.flatMap((weft) => weft.anchors),
  )

## Boxes that point to chapters

A box of the `Chapter` kind points to a chapter, the one its name titles, as the chapter "The leaf tokens" describes. The box stands where a thought leaves its section for a chapter of its own, and it often comes before the chapter does. So a name that finds the box finds the chapter once it is written, and finds the box itself until then.

An `OpenedBox` holds the lines of a loom that make up one box: the `opener`, the line that opens the box, with its `box` token, the box's `body`, and its `closer`, the line that closes it, when one does. `linesOf` lists a document's lines in the order they are written, its preamble and then each section's two runs. `boxFrom` reads one box from the head of those lines, and `openedBoxesOf` reads them all, with `Array.chop` passing over every line outside a box.

A box's name is also its caption, which the editor and the woven page show with it. `captionOf` reads it, and a box with no name takes its kind for a caption, so a folded note still says what it is.

A `BoxRef` is an opened box that holds a place in the book: one with a name, and the location of its loom. `boxesOf` reads a loom's named boxes, and `chapterBoxesOf` keeps those of the `Chapter` kind. The `Book` holds them by loom, which `boxesIn` reads.

=>

export type OpenedBox = {
  readonly box: BoxToken
  readonly opener: LoomWeft
  readonly body: ReadonlyArray<LoomWeft>
  readonly closer: Option.Option<LoomWeft>
}

export type BoxRef = OpenedBox & {
  readonly module: Location
  readonly name: BoxNameToken
}

const linesOf = (doc: LoomDocument): ReadonlyArray<LoomWeft> => [
  ...doc.preamble,
  ...Array.flatMap(doc.sections, (section) => [...section.preamble, ...section.code]),
]

const closesBox = (weft: LoomWeft): boolean =>
  Option.exists(boxOf(weft), (box) => box.side === 'close')

const boxFrom = (
  lines: Array.NonEmptyReadonlyArray<LoomWeft>,
): readonly [Option.Option<OpenedBox>, ReadonlyArray<LoomWeft>] => {
  const opener = Array.headNonEmpty(lines)
  const rest = Array.tailNonEmpty(lines)
  return Option.match(
    Option.filter(boxOf(opener), (box) => box.side === 'open'),
    {
      onNone: () => [Option.none(), rest],
      onSome: (box) => {
        const end = Option.getOrElse(Array.findFirstIndex(rest, closesBox), () => rest.length)
        return [
          Option.some({
            box,
            opener,
            body: Array.take(rest, end),
            closer: Array.get(rest, end),
          }),
          Array.drop(rest, end + 1),
        ]
      },
    },
  )
}

export const openedBoxesOf = (doc: LoomDocument): ReadonlyArray<OpenedBox> =>
  Array.getSomes(Array.chop(linesOf(doc), boxFrom))

export const captionOf = (opened: OpenedBox): string =>
  Option.getOrElse(
    Option.map(Option.fromNullishOr(opened.box.name), (name) => name.value),
    () => boxKind(opened.box),
  )

const boxesOf = (module: LoomModule): ReadonlyArray<BoxRef> =>
  Array.getSomes(
    Array.map(openedBoxesOf(module.doc), (opened) =>
      Option.map(Option.fromNullishOr(opened.box.name), (name): BoxRef => ({
        ...opened,
        module: module.path,
        name,
      })),
    ),
  )

const chapterBoxesOf = (module: LoomModule): ReadonlyArray<BoxRef> =>
  Array.filter(boxesOf(module), (ref) => boxKind(ref.box) === chapterKind)

const boxesIn = (book: Book, module: Location): ReadonlyArray<BoxRef> =>
  Option.getOrElse(Option.fromNullishOr(book.boxes.get(module)), () => [])

~

`boxChapter` finds the chapter a box points to. It looks first among the chapters of the box's own part, where the editor opens a new chapter, and then across the book. `boxNamed` finds a chapter box of one loom by its name. `boxPlace` is the place a box stands for in a lookup: its chapter once the chapter is written, and the box itself until then. `boxTrail` is the lookup's step among the chapter boxes of an anchor's own loom, which `trailsOf` takes after the loom's sections and before the book's chapters. So `::[Rooms]` beside a box `::: Rooms {Chapter}` resolves before the chapter exists and after.

=>

const boxChapter = (book: Book, ref: BoxRef): Option.Option<Chapter> => {
  const titled = (chapter: Chapter): boolean => chapter.title === ref.name.value
  return Option.orElse(
    Option.flatMap(chapterOf(book, ref.module), (own) =>
      Array.head(chaptersWithin(book, Option.some(own.part), titled)),
    ),
    () => Array.findFirst(book.chapters, titled),
  )
}

const boxNamed = (book: Book, module: Location, name: string): Option.Option<BoxRef> =>
  Array.findFirst(boxesIn(book, module), (ref) => ref.name.value === name)

const boxPlace = (book: Book, ref: BoxRef): Place =>
  Option.match(boxChapter(book, ref), {
    onNone: () => Place.Box({ ref }),
    onSome: (chapter) => Place.Chapter({ chapter }),
  })

const boxTrail = (book: Book, from: Location, name: string): ReadonlyArray<Trail> =>
  Option.toArray(
    Option.map(boxNamed(book, from, name), (ref): Trail => [
      { segment: whole(name), place: boxPlace(book, ref), numbered: false },
    ]),
  )

~

The navigation reads a box the way it reads a contents entry. `boxLocation` is where a box's name stands, and `namesBox` tests whether a place is one box. `boxesNaming` finds every chapter box that points to one chapter, so a reference to the chapter lists the boxes, and a rename of the chapter renames them with it.

=>

const boxLocation = (ref: BoxRef): CorpusLocation => ({
  path: ref.module,
  position: ref.name.position,
})

const namesBox =
  (target: BoxRef) =>
  (place: Place): boolean =>
    place._tag === 'Box' &&
    place.ref.module === target.module &&
    place.ref.name.position.start.offset === target.name.position.start.offset

const boxesNaming = (book: Book, chapter: Chapter): ReadonlyArray<CorpusLocation> =>
  pipe(
    Array.flatten(Array.fromIterable(book.boxes.values())),
    Array.filter((ref) =>
      Option.exists(boxChapter(book, ref), (found) => found.module.path === chapter.module.path),
    ),
    Array.map(boxLocation),
  )

## Reaching through the corpus

A run and a tangle need only part of the corpus: the entry and the sections it composes. `reachable` is the forward reach over the anchor graph — the entry, every module a section in it names through an `::[…]` anchor, and the modules those name in turn. Tangling one file never composes an unrelated loom, and a loom the entry never anchors never fails its build. `transitiveDependents` is the reverse — every module that composes a section in a given file, transitively — so an edit reprojects each file that inlined it.

`dependentsOf` runs the same reverse reach from several files at once. The compiler uses it to find what a batch of changed files touches, such as the dozens a `git checkout` rewrites, and it reads the anchor graph once for the whole batch rather than once for each file. It returns the dependents alone, never the files it started from. `transitiveDependents` is `dependentsOf` started from one file.

`reach` grows a set along one direction of the edges until it adds nothing more, guarding a cycle so the walk ends. `anchorEdges` reads the corpus once. Each anchor a module carries becomes an edge to every module its name leads to: the one it resolves to, or each candidate of an ambiguous name. Each chapter box becomes an edge to the chapter it points to, so a change to the chapter's opening reaches the loom whose box holds a copy of it. A change to any of those modules then reaches the anchor's own loom, and a tangle judges an ambiguous name with all of its candidates in view. `moduleOf` reads the module a trail ends in.

=>

const reach = (
  seeds: ReadonlyArray<Location>,
  step: (module: Location) => ReadonlyArray<Location>,
): ReadonlySet<Location> => {
  const grow = (acc: ReadonlySet<Location>, frontier: ReadonlyArray<Location>): ReadonlySet<Location> => {
    const next = pipe(
      frontier,
      Array.flatMap(step),
      Array.filter((p) => !acc.has(p)),
      Array.dedupe,
    )
    return next.length === 0 ? acc : grow(new Set([...acc, ...next]), next)
  }
  return grow(new Set(seeds), seeds)
}

const moduleOf = (trail: Trail): Option.Option<Location> =>
  Match.value(Array.lastNonEmpty(trail).place).pipe(
    Match.tag('Section', ({ ref }) => Option.some(ref.module)),
    Match.tag('Chapter', ({ chapter }) => Option.some(chapter.module.path)),
    Match.tag('Part', () => Option.none<Location>()),
    Match.tag('Box', () => Option.none<Location>()),
    Match.exhaustive,
  )

const anchorEdges = (
  modules: Modules,
): ReadonlyArray<{ readonly owner: Location; readonly target: Location }> => {
  const book = bookOf(modules)
  return pipe(
    Array.fromIterable(modules.values()),
    Array.flatMap((m) => [
      ...pipe(
        m.doc.sections,
        Array.flatMap(anchorsOf),
        Array.flatMap((anchor) =>
          Array.getSomes(
            Array.map(anchorTrails(book, m.path, anchor), (trail) =>
              Option.map(moduleOf(trail), (target) => ({ owner: m.path, target })),
            ),
          ),
        ),
      ),
      ...Array.getSomes(
        Array.map(boxesIn(book, m.path), (ref) =>
          Option.map(boxChapter(book, ref), (chapter) => ({
            owner: m.path,
            target: chapter.module.path,
          })),
        ),
      ),
    ]),
  )
}

export const reachable = (modules: Modules, entry: Location): ReadonlyArray<Location> => {
  const edges = anchorEdges(modules)
  return Array.fromIterable(
    reach([entry], (module) =>
      Array.getSomes(
        Array.map(edges, (edge) =>
          edge.owner === module ? Option.some(edge.target) : Option.none(),
        ),
      ),
    ),
  )
}

export const dependentsOf = (
  modules: Modules,
  entries: ReadonlyArray<Location>,
): ReadonlyArray<Location> => {
  const edges = anchorEdges(modules)
  const seeds = new Set(entries)
  return pipe(
    Array.fromIterable(
      reach(entries, (module) =>
        Array.getSomes(
          Array.map(edges, (edge) =>
            edge.target === module ? Option.some(edge.owner) : Option.none(),
          ),
        ),
      ),
    ),
    Array.filter((path) => !seeds.has(path)),
  )
}

export const transitiveDependents = (modules: Modules, entry: Location): ReadonlyArray<Location> =>
  dependentsOf(modules, [entry])

## Navigation over the corpus

An anchor reaches a section, and a warp anchor reaches the value warp it names — the editor follows both, and `symbolAt` decides which. It reads the kind under a cursor from `LoomSymbol`'s table, so a section anchor resolves a title across the corpus while a warp anchor resolves a name within its own section, each verb dispatching on the same classifier the mappings and colours read. A `CorpusLocation` is where a step lands: the module and the span, in the source's own offsets, which the editor turns into its own coordinates.

=>

export type CorpusLocation = { readonly path: Location; readonly position: Position }

~

Each step starts from the node under a cursor. `anchorAt` finds the anchor whose `::[…]` span covers a source offset. `titledSectionAt` finds the heading whose title covers it, and `sectionRefAt` pairs that section with its module.

=>

const anchorAt = (modules: Modules, path: Location, offset: number): Option.Option<WarpAnchorToken> =>
  pipe(
    Option.fromNullishOr(modules.get(path)),
    Option.flatMap((m) =>
      Array.findFirst(
        Array.flatMap(m.doc.sections, anchorsOf),
        (anchor) => anchor.position.start.offset <= offset && offset <= anchor.position.end.offset,
      ),
    ),
  )

const titledSectionAt = (
  modules: Modules,
  path: Location,
  offset: number,
): Option.Option<LoomSection> =>
  pipe(
    Option.fromNullishOr(modules.get(path)),
    Option.flatMap((m) =>
      Array.findFirst(m.doc.sections, (section) => {
        const title = section.heading.title
        return (
          title !== undefined &&
          title.position.start.offset <= offset &&
          offset <= title.position.end.offset
        )
      }),
    ),
  )

const sectionRefAt = (modules: Modules, path: Location, offset: number): Option.Option<SectionRef> =>
  Option.map(titledSectionAt(modules, path, offset), (section) => ({
    module: path,
    section,
  }))

~

A resolved name then locates its declaration and every anchor that names it. `titleLocation` points at a section's heading title, and `chapterLocation` at the title a chapter declares.

An anchor that holds a path names several places, so the navigation reads the one under the cursor. `segmentPosition` turns a segment's offsets inside the anchor's name into a span of the source, moving the name's own start by `shifted`. `stepAt` picks the step whose segment covers the cursor, or the last step when the cursor sits on a bracket. `anchorStepAt` reads the step under a cursor in an anchor. A `Mention` is a place together with whether it was named by number, and `mentionAt` reads the one under a cursor: the section of a heading, or the step of an anchor.

`anchorsNaming` gathers every anchor across the corpus with a step that a predicate accepts, each by the span a caller picks: the whole anchor for a reference, or the one segment for a rename. `stepsOf` gives an anchor's steps, and only an anchor that resolves to one trail has any, since an ambiguous anchor names nothing for certain. `namesSection` accepts one section, and `namesChapter` accepts one chapter. An anchor names a section only through a segment that writes the section's own title. An anchor that reaches a chapter's opening section through the chapter's title names the chapter, not the section.

=>

type SpanOf = (anchor: WarpAnchorToken, segment: Segment) => Position

const titleLocation = (ref: SectionRef): Option.Option<CorpusLocation> =>
  Option.map(Option.fromNullishOr(ref.section.heading.title), (title): CorpusLocation => ({
    path: ref.module,
    position: title.position,
  }))

const chapterLocation = (chapter: Chapter): Option.Option<CorpusLocation> =>
  Option.map(chapterTitleToken(chapter.module), (title): CorpusLocation => ({
    path: chapter.module.path,
    position: title.position,
  }))

const shifted = (point: Point, by: number): Point =>
  Option.match(Option.fromNullishOr(point.column), {
    onNone: () => ({ ...point, offset: point.offset + by }),
    onSome: (column) => ({ ...point, offset: point.offset + by, column: column + by }),
  })

const segmentPosition: SpanOf = (anchor, segment) => ({
  start: shifted(anchor.name.position.start, segment.start),
  end: shifted(anchor.name.position.start, segment.end),
})

const wholeAnchor: SpanOf = (anchor) => anchor.position

const stepAt = (trail: Trail, anchor: WarpAnchorToken, offset: number): Step =>
  Option.getOrElse(
    Array.findFirst(trail, (step) => covers(segmentPosition(anchor, step.segment), offset)),
    () => Array.lastNonEmpty(trail),
  )

const anchorStepAt = (
  book: Book,
  modules: Modules,
  path: Location,
  offset: number,
): Option.Option<Step> =>
  Option.flatMap(anchorAt(modules, path, offset), (anchor) =>
    Option.map(soleTrail(anchorTrails(book, path, anchor)), (trail) =>
      stepAt(trail, anchor, offset),
    ),
  )

type Mention = { readonly place: Place; readonly numbered: boolean }

const mentionAt = (
  book: Book,
  modules: Modules,
  path: Location,
  offset: number,
): Option.Option<Mention> =>
  Option.orElse(
    Option.map(sectionRefAt(modules, path, offset), (ref): Mention => ({
      place: Place.Section({ ref }),
      numbered: false,
    })),
    () => anchorStepAt(book, modules, path, offset),
  )

const stepsOf = (book: Book, from: Location, anchor: WarpAnchorToken): ReadonlyArray<Step> =>
  Option.match(soleTrail(anchorTrails(book, from, anchor)), {
    onNone: (): ReadonlyArray<Step> => [],
    onSome: (trail) => trail,
  })

const anchorsNaming = (
  book: Book,
  modules: Modules,
  names: (step: Step) => boolean,
  spanOf: SpanOf,
): ReadonlyArray<CorpusLocation> =>
  Array.flatMap(Array.fromIterable(modules.values()), (m) =>
    pipe(
      m.doc.sections,
      Array.flatMap(anchorsOf),
      Array.flatMap((anchor) =>
        pipe(
          stepsOf(book, m.path, anchor),
          Array.filter(names),
          Array.map((step): CorpusLocation => ({
            path: m.path,
            position: spanOf(anchor, step.segment),
          })),
        ),
      ),
    ),
  )

const namesSection =
  (target: SectionRef) =>
  (place: Place): boolean =>
    place._tag === 'Section' &&
    place.ref.module === target.module &&
    place.ref.section === target.section

const namesChapter =
  (chapter: Chapter) =>
  (place: Place): boolean =>
    place._tag === 'Chapter' && place.chapter.module.path === chapter.module.path

~

`sectionDefinitionAt` follows an anchor to what the segment under the cursor names: a section's heading, or the title a chapter declares. `sectionNames` gathers a section's heading beside every anchor that names it. A chapter whose frontmatter declares no title takes its title from its first heading, and that heading then titles the chapter as well as the section. So its names take in the chapter's too: the contents entries that list the chapter, the boxes that point to it, and the anchors that name the chapter by its title. `chapterTitledBy` finds the chapter a heading titles this way. `placeNames` gathers the names of a mention. A chapter named by its title goes to `chapterNames`, and a chapter named by its number goes to `chapterNumbers`, which the contents navigation below defines. So a rename from a title rewrites titles, and a rename from a number renumbers. `placeNamesAt` reads the mention under a cursor and gathers its names. The exported verbs call these once `symbolAt` names the cursor a section.

=>

const sectionDefinitionAt = (
  corpus: LoomCorpusAst,
  path: Location,
  offset: number,
): Option.Option<CorpusLocation> =>
  Option.flatMap(anchorStepAt(bookOf(corpus.modules), corpus.modules, path, offset), ({ place }) =>
    Match.value(place).pipe(
      Match.tag('Section', ({ ref }) => titleLocation(ref)),
      Match.tag('Chapter', ({ chapter }) => chapterLocation(chapter)),
      Match.tag('Box', ({ ref }) => Option.some(boxLocation(ref))),
      Match.tag('Part', () => Option.none<CorpusLocation>()),
      Match.exhaustive,
    ),
  )

const chapterTitledBy = (book: Book, ref: SectionRef): Option.Option<Chapter> =>
  Array.findFirst(
    book.chapters,
    (chapter) =>
      chapter.module.path === ref.module &&
      chapter.module.doc.frontmatter?.title === undefined &&
      Option.exists(openingSectionOf(chapter.module), (opening) => opening === ref.section),
  )

const sectionNames = (
  book: Book,
  modules: Modules,
  ref: SectionRef,
  spanOf: SpanOf,
): ReadonlyArray<CorpusLocation> => [
  ...Option.toArray(titleLocation(ref)),
  ...anchorsNaming(book, modules, (step) => namesSection(ref)(step.place), spanOf),
  ...Option.match(chapterTitledBy(book, ref), {
    onNone: (): ReadonlyArray<CorpusLocation> => [],
    onSome: (chapter) => [
      ...entriesNaming(book, modules, chapter),
      ...boxesNaming(book, chapter),
      ...anchorsNaming(
        book,
        modules,
        (step) => !step.numbered && namesChapter(chapter)(step.place),
        spanOf,
      ),
    ],
  }),
]

const placeNames = (
  book: Book,
  modules: Modules,
  mention: Mention,
  spanOf: SpanOf,
): ReadonlyArray<CorpusLocation> =>
  Match.value(mention.place).pipe(
    Match.tag('Section', ({ ref }) => sectionNames(book, modules, ref, spanOf)),
    Match.tag('Chapter', ({ chapter }) =>
      mention.numbered
        ? chapterNumbers(book, modules, chapter, spanOf)
        : chapterNames(book, modules, chapter, spanOf),
    ),
    Match.tag('Box', ({ ref }) => [
      boxLocation(ref),
      ...anchorsNaming(book, modules, (step) => namesBox(ref)(step.place), spanOf),
    ]),
    Match.tag('Part', (): ReadonlyArray<CorpusLocation> => []),
    Match.exhaustive,
  )

const placeNamesAt = (
  corpus: LoomCorpusAst,
  path: Location,
  offset: number,
  spanOf: SpanOf,
): ReadonlyArray<CorpusLocation> => {
  const book = bookOf(corpus.modules)
  return Option.match(mentionAt(book, corpus.modules, path, offset), {
    onNone: (): ReadonlyArray<CorpusLocation> => [],
    onSome: (mention) => placeNames(book, corpus.modules, mention, spanOf),
  })
}

~

A warp anchor resolves within its section, not across the corpus. `::[ratio]` names the value warp `ratio` its section declares, or the document's when the section declares none — the same scope `symbolsOf` classifies a warp anchor by, so the editor and the classifier agree. `valueWarpsIn` reads a preamble's value warps, the lang warp excepted. `sectionCovering` gives the section an offset sits in, and `warpDefFor` picks the warp a name sees there: the section's own, else the document's, so a section warp shadows the document's. `warpNameAt` reads the name under a cursor, whether a `{{ratio = …}}` definition or a `::[ratio]` anchor. `warpBindingAt` ties them together — the definition and every anchor that binds to it — and `warpDefLocation` and `warpReferences` shape that binding into the definition span and the reference spans a caller picks.

=>

const covers = (position: Position, offset: number): boolean =>
  position.start.offset <= offset && offset <= position.end.offset

const valueWarpsIn = (
  preamble: ReadonlyArray<{ readonly warps: ReadonlyArray<WarpToken> }>,
): ReadonlyArray<WarpToken> =>
  Array.filter(
    Array.flatMap(preamble, (weft) => weft.warps),
    (warp) => warp.name.value !== 'lang',
  )

const allValueWarpsOf = (doc: LoomDocument): ReadonlyArray<WarpToken> => [
  ...valueWarpsIn(doc.preamble),
  ...Array.flatMap(doc.sections, (section) => valueWarpsIn(section.preamble)),
]

const allAnchorsOf = (doc: LoomDocument): ReadonlyArray<WarpAnchorToken> => [
  ...Array.flatMap(doc.preamble, (weft) => weft.anchors),
  ...Array.flatMap(doc.sections, anchorsOf),
]

const sectionCovering = (module: LoomModule, offset: number): Option.Option<LoomSection> =>
  Array.findFirst(module.doc.sections, (section) => covers(section.position, offset))

const warpDefFor = (
  module: LoomModule,
  section: Option.Option<LoomSection>,
  name: string,
): Option.Option<WarpToken> =>
  pipe(
    section,
    Option.flatMap((s) => Array.findFirst(valueWarpsIn(s.preamble), (w) => w.name.value === name)),
    Option.orElse(() =>
      Array.findFirst(valueWarpsIn(module.doc.preamble), (w) => w.name.value === name),
    ),
  )

const warpNameAt = (module: LoomModule, offset: number): Option.Option<string> =>
  Option.orElse(
    Option.map(
      Array.findFirst(allValueWarpsOf(module.doc), (w) => covers(w.name.position, offset)),
      (w) => w.name.value,
    ),
    () =>
      Option.map(
        Array.findFirst(allAnchorsOf(module.doc), (a) => covers(a.name.position, offset)),
        (a) => a.name.value,
      ),
  )

const warpBindingAt = (
  module: LoomModule,
  offset: number,
): Option.Option<{
  readonly def: WarpToken
  readonly anchors: ReadonlyArray<WarpAnchorToken>
}> =>
  pipe(
    warpNameAt(module, offset),
    Option.flatMap((name) =>
      Option.map(warpDefFor(module, sectionCovering(module, offset), name), (def) => ({
        def,
        anchors: Array.filter(
          allAnchorsOf(module.doc),
          (anchor) =>
            anchor.name.value === name &&
            Option.getOrUndefined(
              warpDefFor(module, sectionCovering(module, anchor.position.start.offset), name),
            ) === def,
        ),
      })),
    ),
  )

const warpDefLocation = (
  module: LoomModule,
  path: Location,
  offset: number,
): Option.Option<CorpusLocation> =>
  Option.map(warpBindingAt(module, offset), (binding): CorpusLocation => ({
    path,
    position: binding.def.name.position,
  }))

const warpReferences = (
  module: LoomModule,
  path: Location,
  offset: number,
  spanOf: (anchor: WarpAnchorToken) => Position,
): ReadonlyArray<CorpusLocation> =>
  Option.match(warpBindingAt(module, offset), {
    onNone: () => [],
    onSome: (binding) => [
      { path, position: binding.def.name.position },
      ...Array.map(binding.anchors, (anchor): CorpusLocation => ({
        path,
        position: spanOf(anchor),
      })),
    ],
  })

~

A table-of-contents entry names a chapter by its title, under the part line above it. A chapter's identity is the title it declares, not its heading, because a heading can differ from the title the book lists, or be missing. `chapterTitleToken` reads that identity: the frontmatter `title`, or the first heading for a loom that carries no frontmatter. `firstHeadingTitleOf` reads that heading from the loom's opening section.

A `PlacedEntry` is an entry together with the part it stands under. `placedEntriesOf` walks a contents section's lines in order and carries the last part line it passed, and `tocPartRef` reads that line's `Part R — name` label into the part's number and name. `entryChapter` resolves an entry to the chapter that carries its title within its part, so two chapters called `Setup` in two parts each answer to their own entry. An entry under no part line matches the title alone.

`chapterAt` reads the chapter under a cursor: the chapter a contents entry resolves to, the chapter a chapter box's name points to, or the chapter whose frontmatter title the cursor sits on. `chapterDefinitionAt` follows it to the title the chapter declares. `unresolvedTocEntriesIn` is the other side: every entry that resolves to no chapter, for the diagnostics to fault.

`entriesNaming` finds every contents entry that resolves to one chapter, and `boxesNaming` every chapter box that points to it. `chapterNames` gathers the chapter's declared title, those entries and boxes, and every anchor that names the chapter by its title, so a reference and a rename move the whole name together. `chapterNamesAt` reads the chapter under a cursor and gathers its names.

A chapter's number is a name of its own. `chapterNumbers` gathers the `Chapter N` the chapter's frontmatter declares and every anchor that names the chapter by that number, so renumbering a chapter rewrites each numbered reference in the same edit. A contents entry is listed by title, so its own ordinal is no reference, and the table of contents is generated anew from the frontmatter. `chapterNumbersAt` gathers them for the chapter whose frontmatter number the cursor sits on.

A part resolves the same way, keyed by its number rather than its title. A part is announced once, in its opening chapter's standalone `Part R: name`, whose `partName` is the part's identity; `partOpenerIndex` maps each part number to that announcement. The number under a cursor comes from a chapter's `Part R` through `frontmatterPartNumberAt`, or from a contents part heading through `tocPartNumberAt`, which reads it out of the `Part R — name` label; `partNumberAt` takes either. `partDefinitionAt` follows the number to the opener, and `partGroup` gathers the whole part — the announcement, every chapter's `Part R`, and every contents heading that names it — which `partGroupAt` reads from the cursor.

=>

const firstHeadingTitleOf = (module: LoomModule): Option.Option<HeadingTitleToken> =>
  Option.flatMap(openingSectionOf(module), (section) => Option.fromNullishOr(section.heading.title))

const chapterTitleToken = (
  module: LoomModule,
): Option.Option<{ readonly source: string; readonly position: Position }> =>
  Option.orElse(Option.fromNullishOr(module.doc.frontmatter?.title), () =>
    firstHeadingTitleOf(module),
  )

const tocEntriesOf = (module: LoomModule): ReadonlyArray<TocWeft> =>
  Array.flatMap(module.doc.sections, (section) => section.entries ?? [])

type PlacedEntry = { readonly title: TocTitleToken; readonly part: Option.Option<PartRef> }

const tocPartRef = (part: TocPartToken): PartRef =>
  Option.match(Option.fromNullishOr(partNumberMarker.exec(part.value)), {
    onNone: () => ({ number: Option.none(), name: Option.some(part.value.trim()) }),
    onSome: (match) => ({
      number: Option.fromNullishOr(match[1]),
      name: Option.fromNullishOr(/[—–-]\s*(.+)$/.exec(part.value)?.[1]?.trim()),
    }),
  })

const placedEntriesOf = (module: LoomModule): ReadonlyArray<PlacedEntry> =>
  Array.flatMap(module.doc.sections, (section) =>
    Array.getSomes(
      Array.mapAccum(
        section.entries ?? [],
        Option.none<PartRef>(),
        (part, entry): readonly [Option.Option<PartRef>, Option.Option<PlacedEntry>] =>
          Option.match(Option.fromNullishOr(entry.part), {
            onSome: (token) => [Option.some(tocPartRef(token)), Option.none()],
            onNone: () => [
              part,
              Option.map(Option.fromNullishOr(entry.title), (title) => ({ title, part })),
            ],
          }),
      )[1],
    ),
  )

const entryChapter = (book: Book, placed: PlacedEntry): Option.Option<Chapter> =>
  Array.findFirst(
    book.chapters,
    (chapter) =>
      chapter.title === placed.title.value &&
      Option.match(placed.part, {
        onNone: () => true,
        onSome: (part) => samePart(chapter.part, part),
      }),
  )

const chapterAt = (
  book: Book,
  modules: Modules,
  path: Location,
  offset: number,
): Option.Option<Chapter> =>
  Option.flatMap(Option.fromNullishOr(modules.get(path)), (module) =>
    Option.orElse(
      Option.flatMap(
        Array.findFirst(placedEntriesOf(module), (placed) => covers(placed.title.position, offset)),
        (placed) => entryChapter(book, placed),
      ),
      () =>
        Option.orElse(
          Option.flatMap(
            Array.findFirst(boxesIn(book, path), (ref) => covers(ref.name.position, offset)),
            (ref) => boxChapter(book, ref),
          ),
          () =>
            pipe(
              Option.fromNullishOr(module.doc.frontmatter?.title),
              Option.filter((title) => covers(title.position, offset)),
              Option.flatMap(() => chapterOf(book, path)),
            ),
        ),
    ),
  )

const chapterDefinitionAt = (
  corpus: LoomCorpusAst,
  path: Location,
  offset: number,
): Option.Option<CorpusLocation> =>
  Option.flatMap(chapterAt(bookOf(corpus.modules), corpus.modules, path, offset), chapterLocation)

const entriesNaming = (
  book: Book,
  modules: Modules,
  chapter: Chapter,
): ReadonlyArray<CorpusLocation> =>
  Array.flatMap(Array.fromIterable(modules.values()), (module) =>
    pipe(
      placedEntriesOf(module),
      Array.filter((placed) =>
        Option.exists(
          entryChapter(book, placed),
          (found) => found.module.path === chapter.module.path,
        ),
      ),
      Array.map((placed): CorpusLocation => ({
        path: module.path,
        position: placed.title.position,
      })),
    ),
  )

const chapterNames = (
  book: Book,
  modules: Modules,
  chapter: Chapter,
  spanOf: SpanOf,
): ReadonlyArray<CorpusLocation> => [
  ...Option.toArray(chapterLocation(chapter)),
  ...entriesNaming(book, modules, chapter),
  ...boxesNaming(book, chapter),
  ...anchorsNaming(
    book,
    modules,
    (step) => !step.numbered && namesChapter(chapter)(step.place),
    spanOf,
  ),
]

const chapterNamesAt = (
  corpus: LoomCorpusAst,
  path: Location,
  offset: number,
  spanOf: SpanOf,
): ReadonlyArray<CorpusLocation> => {
  const book = bookOf(corpus.modules)
  return Option.match(chapterAt(book, corpus.modules, path, offset), {
    onNone: (): ReadonlyArray<CorpusLocation> => [],
    onSome: (chapter) => chapterNames(book, corpus.modules, chapter, spanOf),
  })
}

const chapterNumbers = (
  book: Book,
  modules: Modules,
  chapter: Chapter,
  spanOf: SpanOf,
): ReadonlyArray<CorpusLocation> => [
  ...Option.toArray(
    Option.map(
      Option.fromNullishOr(chapter.module.doc.frontmatter?.chapter),
      (number): CorpusLocation => ({ path: chapter.module.path, position: number.position }),
    ),
  ),
  ...anchorsNaming(
    book,
    modules,
    (step) => step.numbered && namesChapter(chapter)(step.place),
    spanOf,
  ),
]

const chapterNumbersAt = (
  corpus: LoomCorpusAst,
  path: Location,
  spanOf: SpanOf,
): ReadonlyArray<CorpusLocation> => {
  const book = bookOf(corpus.modules)
  return Option.match(chapterOf(book, path), {
    onNone: (): ReadonlyArray<CorpusLocation> => [],
    onSome: (chapter) => chapterNumbers(book, corpus.modules, chapter, spanOf),
  })
}

export const unresolvedTocEntriesIn = (
  corpus: LoomCorpusAst,
  module: LoomModule,
): ReadonlyArray<TocTitleToken> => {
  const book = bookOf(corpus.modules)
  return pipe(
    placedEntriesOf(module),
    Array.filter((placed) => Option.isNone(entryChapter(book, placed))),
    Array.map((placed) => placed.title),
  )
}

const partOpenerIndex = (modules: Modules): ReadonlyMap<string, CorpusLocation> =>
  pipe(
    Array.fromIterable(modules.values()),
    Array.map((module) =>
      pipe(
        Option.fromNullishOr(module.doc.frontmatter),
        Option.flatMap((fm) =>
          Option.all({
            part: Option.fromNullishOr(fm.part),
            partName: Option.fromNullishOr(fm.partName),
          }),
        ),
        Option.map(
          ({ part, partName }) =>
            [part.value, { path: module.path, position: partName.position }] as const,
        ),
      ),
    ),
    Array.getSomes,
    Array.reduce(new Map<string, CorpusLocation>(), (index, [num, location]) =>
      index.has(num) ? index : new Map(index).set(num, location),
    ),
  )

const onToken = (token: { readonly position: Position } | undefined, offset: number): boolean =>
  Option.match(Option.fromNullishOr(token), {
    onNone: () => false,
    onSome: (t) => covers(t.position, offset),
  })

const frontmatterPartNumberAt = (module: LoomModule, offset: number): Option.Option<string> =>
  pipe(
    Option.fromNullishOr(module.doc.frontmatter),
    Option.filter((fm) => onToken(fm.part, offset) || onToken(fm.partName, offset)),
    Option.flatMap((fm) => Option.fromNullishOr(fm.part)),
    Option.map((part) => part.value),
  )

const tocPartsOf = (module: LoomModule): ReadonlyArray<TocPartToken> =>
  Array.getSomes(Array.map(tocEntriesOf(module), (entry) => Option.fromNullishOr(entry.part)))

const partNumberMarker = /^Part\s+(\S+)/

const tocPartNumberAt = (module: LoomModule, offset: number): Option.Option<string> =>
  pipe(
    Array.findFirst(tocPartsOf(module), (part) => covers(part.position, offset)),
    Option.flatMap((part) => Option.fromNullishOr(partNumberMarker.exec(part.value)?.[1])),
  )

const partNumberAt = (modules: Modules, path: Location, offset: number): Option.Option<string> =>
  pipe(
    Option.fromNullishOr(modules.get(path)),
    Option.flatMap((module) =>
      Option.orElse(frontmatterPartNumberAt(module, offset), () => tocPartNumberAt(module, offset)),
    ),
  )

const partDefinitionAt = (
  corpus: LoomCorpusAst,
  path: Location,
  offset: number,
): Option.Option<CorpusLocation> =>
  Option.flatMap(partNumberAt(corpus.modules, path, offset), (num) =>
    Option.fromNullishOr(partOpenerIndex(corpus.modules).get(num)),
  )

const frontmatterPartLocations = (module: LoomModule, num: string): ReadonlyArray<CorpusLocation> =>
  pipe(
    Option.fromNullishOr(module.doc.frontmatter?.part),
    Option.filter((part) => part.value === num),
    Option.map((part) => ({ path: module.path, position: part.position })),
    Option.toArray,
  )

const tocPartLocations = (module: LoomModule, num: string): ReadonlyArray<CorpusLocation> =>
  Array.getSomes(
    Array.map(tocPartsOf(module), (part) =>
      pipe(
        Option.fromNullishOr(partNumberMarker.exec(part.value)?.[1]),
        Option.filter((n) => n === num),
        Option.map(() => ({ path: module.path, position: part.position })),
      ),
    ),
  )

const partGroup = (modules: Modules, num: string): ReadonlyArray<CorpusLocation> => [
  ...Option.toArray(Option.fromNullishOr(partOpenerIndex(modules).get(num))),
  ...Array.flatMap(Array.fromIterable(modules.values()), (module) => [
    ...frontmatterPartLocations(module, num),
    ...tocPartLocations(module, num),
  ]),
]

const partGroupAt = (
  corpus: LoomCorpusAst,
  path: Location,
  offset: number,
): ReadonlyArray<CorpusLocation> =>
  Option.match(partNumberAt(corpus.modules, path, offset), {
    onNone: (): ReadonlyArray<CorpusLocation> => [],
    onSome: (num) => partGroup(corpus.modules, num),
  })

~

`definitionAt` follows the reference under a cursor to its definition — a section anchor to the heading or chapter title its segment names, a warp anchor to its warp, a contents entry, a chapter box's name, or a chapter's own title to that chapter, and a `Part R` or a contents part heading to where the part is announced. `referencesAt` returns every place the thing under the cursor is named — a section and its anchors, a warp and its anchors, a chapter's title with the entries, boxes, and anchors that name it, a chapter's number with the anchors that name it by number, or a part's announcement and every `Part R` and contents heading that names it. Each reads the kind from `symbolAt` and dispatches on it through one `Match`.

=>

export const definitionAt = (
  corpus: LoomCorpusAst,
  path: Location,
  offset: number,
): Option.Option<CorpusLocation> =>
  pipe(
    Option.fromNullishOr(corpus.modules.get(path)),
    Option.flatMap((module) =>
      Option.flatMap(symbolAt(module.doc, offset), (symbol) =>
        Match.value(symbol.kind).pipe(
          Match.when('warpAnchor', () => warpDefLocation(module, path, offset)),
          Match.when('sectionAnchor', () => sectionDefinitionAt(corpus, path, offset)),
          Match.whenOr('tocEntry', 'boxName', 'frontmatterTitle', () =>
            chapterDefinitionAt(corpus, path, offset),
          ),
          Match.whenOr('tocPart', 'frontmatterPart', 'frontmatterPartName', () =>
            partDefinitionAt(corpus, path, offset),
          ),
          Match.orElse(() => Option.none<CorpusLocation>()),
        ),
      ),
    ),
  )

export const referencesAt = (
  corpus: LoomCorpusAst,
  path: Location,
  offset: number,
): ReadonlyArray<CorpusLocation> =>
  Option.match(
    pipe(
      Option.fromNullishOr(corpus.modules.get(path)),
      Option.flatMap((module) =>
        Option.map(symbolAt(module.doc, offset), (symbol) => ({ module, symbol })),
      ),
    ),
    {
      onNone: () => [],
      onSome: ({ module, symbol }) =>
        Match.value(symbol.kind).pipe(
          Match.whenOr('warpAnchor', 'warpDef', () =>
            warpReferences(module, path, offset, (anchor) => anchor.position),
          ),
          Match.whenOr('headingTitle', 'sectionAnchor', () =>
            placeNamesAt(corpus, path, offset, wholeAnchor),
          ),
          Match.whenOr('tocEntry', 'boxName', 'frontmatterTitle', () =>
            chapterNamesAt(corpus, path, offset, wholeAnchor),
          ),
          Match.when('frontmatterMembership', () => chapterNumbersAt(corpus, path, wholeAnchor)),
          Match.whenOr('tocPart', 'frontmatterPart', 'frontmatterPartName', () =>
            partGroupAt(corpus, path, offset),
          ),
          Match.orElse((): ReadonlyArray<CorpusLocation> => []),
        ),
    },
  )

## Renaming a section

A rename is one edit across the scope a name spans, so the name and its references never drift. A section's title moves with every anchor that names the section. A warp's definition moves with every anchor that binds to it. A chapter's declared title moves with every contents entry and every anchor that names the chapter by title, and its number moves with every anchor that names it by number. The exported `renameAt` reads `symbolAt` and gathers these spans through the same functions the references use, with `segmentPosition` as the span. Each span is one name alone: a title, or one segment inside a `::[…]`. So the brackets, the other names of a path, and any `(path)` stay put. A part is not renamed: its name sits inside the contents label, and its chapters refer to it by a bare number.

=>

export const renameAt = (
  corpus: LoomCorpusAst,
  path: Location,
  offset: number,
): ReadonlyArray<CorpusLocation> =>
  Option.match(
    pipe(
      Option.fromNullishOr(corpus.modules.get(path)),
      Option.flatMap((module) =>
        Option.map(symbolAt(module.doc, offset), (symbol) => ({ module, symbol })),
      ),
    ),
    {
      onNone: () => [],
      onSome: ({ module, symbol }) =>
        Match.value(symbol.kind).pipe(
          Match.whenOr('warpAnchor', 'warpDef', () =>
            warpReferences(module, path, offset, (anchor) => anchor.name.position),
          ),
          Match.whenOr('headingTitle', 'sectionAnchor', () =>
            placeNamesAt(corpus, path, offset, segmentPosition),
          ),
          Match.whenOr('tocEntry', 'boxName', 'frontmatterTitle', () =>
            chapterNamesAt(corpus, path, offset, segmentPosition),
          ),
          Match.when('frontmatterMembership', () =>
            chapterNumbersAt(corpus, path, segmentPosition),
          ),
          Match.orElse((): ReadonlyArray<CorpusLocation> => []),
        ),
    },
  )

~

`navigationRangeAt` is the span a navigable symbol occupies — the whole title, anchor, warp, or `Part R`, read from the same classification the mappings and colours read. It is the range a definition link underlines on hover and the range a rename box covers, so the editor takes the whole name rather than the one word the cursor sits in. The rule is the capability: a kind whose `profileOf` sets `navigation` spans its whole token, and everything that draws a selection reads it here. `renameRangeAt` narrows that to the renameable — the span only where `renameAt` has edits to make — so a part, navigable but not yet renameable, underlines on hover yet opens no rename box.

=>

export const navigationRangeAt = (
  corpus: LoomCorpusAst,
  path: Location,
  offset: number,
): Option.Option<CorpusLocation> =>
  pipe(
    Option.fromNullishOr(corpus.modules.get(path)),
    Option.flatMap((module) => symbolAt(module.doc, offset)),
    Option.filter((symbol) => profileOf(symbol.kind).features.navigation === true),
    Option.map((symbol): CorpusLocation => ({ path, position: symbol.position })),
  )

export const renameRangeAt = (
  corpus: LoomCorpusAst,
  path: Location,
  offset: number,
): Option.Option<CorpusLocation> =>
  Option.filter(
    navigationRangeAt(corpus, path, offset),
    () => renameAt(corpus, path, offset).length > 0,
  )

## Titles beside numbers

A number names a position, not a chapter. When a chapter moves, a numbered reference points at whichever chapter took the number, and it still resolves, so no fault reveals the change. So the editor shows, beside each segment that names a part or a chapter by number, the title it stands for, and a reader sees where the reference points.

`hintsAt` gives those titles for one loom. A `CorpusHint` places a label at an offset in the loom's source, the end of the numbered segment. `labelOf` reads the label a place shows: a chapter's title, or a part's name when the book knows it. A section is never named by number, so it shows none.

=>

export type CorpusHint = { readonly offset: number; readonly label: string }

const labelOf = (place: Place): Option.Option<string> =>
  Match.value(place).pipe(
    Match.tag('Chapter', ({ chapter }) => Option.some(chapter.title)),
    Match.tag('Part', ({ part }) => part.name),
    Match.tag('Section', () => Option.none<string>()),
    Match.tag('Box', () => Option.none<string>()),
    Match.exhaustive,
  )

export const hintsAt = (corpus: LoomCorpusAst, path: Location): ReadonlyArray<CorpusHint> => {
  const book = bookOf(corpus.modules)
  return Option.match(Option.fromNullishOr(corpus.modules.get(path)), {
    onNone: (): ReadonlyArray<CorpusHint> => [],
    onSome: (module) =>
      pipe(
        module.doc.sections,
        Array.flatMap(anchorsOf),
        Array.flatMap((anchor) =>
          pipe(
            stepsOf(book, path, anchor),
            Array.filter((step) => step.numbered),
            Array.map((step) =>
              Option.map(labelOf(step.place), (label) => ({
                offset: segmentPosition(anchor, step.segment).end.offset,
                label,
              })),
            ),
            Array.getSomes,
          ),
        ),
      ),
  })
}

## Braces waiting for a label

An anchor takes a label in braces right after it, as `::[Greeting function]{To be settled}` does. An author types the label one letter at a time, so the editor offers the labels an anchor takes while the cursor stands between the braces. To make that offer, the editor needs the span the chosen label replaces.

The `labelSlotAt` function finds that span in one loom. A `LabelSlot` is the span, the slot a label fills: its `start` and `end` in the loom's source, and whether braces close it. Closed braces form a specifier, and the slot is the label between them. A brace the author has only opened forms nothing yet, because the tokeniser reads an open brace without its close as ordinary text. The slot then runs from just after the brace to the cursor, as long as the cursor stays on the anchor's line. Braces that hold parameters already name their keys, so they hold no slot. The `slotOf` function reads the slot of one anchor, after its target when it has one, and keeps the slot only when the cursor stands inside it.

=>

export type LabelSlot = {
  readonly start: number
  readonly end: number
  readonly closed: boolean
}

const openSlot = (text: string, brace: number, offset: number): Option.Option<LabelSlot> =>
  text[brace] === '{' && brace < offset && !text.slice(brace, offset).includes('\n')
    ? Option.some({ start: brace + 1, end: offset, closed: false })
    : Option.none()

const slotOf =
  (text: string, offset: number) =>
  (anchor: WarpAnchorToken): Option.Option<LabelSlot> =>
    pipe(
      Match.value(anchor.specifier).pipe(
        Match.when({ type: 'Specifier' }, ({ open, close }) =>
          Option.some({
            start: open.position.end.offset,
            end: close.position.start.offset,
            closed: true,
          }),
        ),
        Match.when({ type: 'Parameters' }, () => Option.none<LabelSlot>()),
        Match.when(Match.undefined, () => openSlot(text, anchor.position.end.offset, offset)),
        Match.exhaustive,
      ),
      Option.filter((slot) => slot.start <= offset && offset <= slot.end),
    )

export const labelSlotAt = (
  corpus: LoomCorpusAst,
  path: Location,
  offset: number,
): Option.Option<LabelSlot> =>
  pipe(
    Option.fromNullishOr(corpus.modules.get(path)),
    Option.flatMap((module) =>
      Array.findFirst(allAnchorsOf(module.doc), slotOf(module.text, offset)),
    ),
  )

## Keeping a chapter box in step

A chapter box holds a copy of the chapter's summary, the opening prose under its title. The chapter holds the summary's source, and the box holds the copy, so a reader of the section sees what the chapter is about without leaving it. `openingProseOf` reads a chapter's summary: the lines of its opening section up to its first code, without the boxes among them, since a box never holds another. `outsideBoxes` drops every box from a run of lines, and `stillInBox` follows whether a line stands in one. `textOf` joins lines into text, and `tidy` evens out what a copy may change without meaning to: the kind of line ending, the blank lines between paragraphs, and the space at either end.

=>

const tidy = (text: string): string =>
  text
    .replace(/\r\n?/g, '\n')
    .replace(/\n{3,}/g, '\n\n')
    .trim()

const textOf = (lines: ReadonlyArray<LoomWeft>): string =>
  tidy(
    Array.join(
      Array.map(lines, (line) => line.source),
      '',
    ),
  )

const stillInBox = (inBox: boolean, box: BoxToken): boolean =>
  Match.value(box.side).pipe(
    Match.when('open', () => true),
    Match.when('close', () => false),
    Match.when('stray', () => inBox),
    Match.exhaustive,
  )

const outsideBoxes = (lines: ReadonlyArray<LoomWeft>): ReadonlyArray<LoomWeft> =>
  Array.getSomes(
    Array.mapAccum(lines, false, (inBox, line): readonly [boolean, Option.Option<LoomWeft>] =>
      Option.match(boxOf(line), {
        onNone: () => [inBox, inBox ? Option.none() : Option.some(line)],
        onSome: (box) => [stillInBox(inBox, box), Option.none()],
      }),
    )[1],
  )

const openingProseOf = (module: LoomModule): string =>
  Option.match(openingSectionOf(module), {
    onNone: () => '',
    onSome: (section) =>
      textOf(
        outsideBoxes(
          Array.takeWhile(
            [...section.preamble, ...section.code],
            (line) => line.type !== 'ArrowWeft',
          ),
        ),
      ),
  })

~

A box may point to a chapter nobody has written. The editor then offers to write it, and `openingOf` gives the `Opening` it writes: the new loom's `file` and its `text`. The new chapter stands beside the loom of the box, in the box's own part, and takes the part's next number, which `nextNumber` finds. Its file is named for that number and for its title, which `slugOf` turns into lowercase words joined by hyphens, as `07-rooms.loom`. A part without numbers names its chapters by title alone, so its new chapter takes neither a number nor one in its file name. `membership` writes the identity line in the form the part already uses. The new chapter's status is `Canvas`, as the chapter "Canvases" describes. It keeps the language of the box's chapter, and its opening prose is the box's body, so the copy and its source agree from the start.

=>

export type Opening = { readonly file: string; readonly text: string }

const noPart: PartRef = { number: Option.none(), name: Option.none() }

const slugOf = (title: string): string =>
  title
    .toLowerCase()
    .replace(/['’]/g, '')
    .replace(/[^a-z0-9]+/g, '-')
    .replace(/^-+|-+$/g, '')

const folderOf = (path: Location): string => path.slice(0, path.lastIndexOf('/') + 1)

const numberOf = (chapter: Chapter): number =>
  Option.getOrElse(
    Option.map(chapter.number, (written) => Number.parseInt(written, 10) || 0),
    () => 0,
  )

const nextNumber = (book: Book, part: PartRef): Option.Option<number> =>
  Option.map(
    part.number,
    () =>
      1 +
      Math.max(
        0,
        ...Array.map(
          chaptersWithin(book, Option.some(part), () => true),
          numberOf,
        ),
      ),
  )

const membership = (
  part: PartRef,
  number: Option.Option<number>,
  title: string,
): ReadonlyArray<string> =>
  Option.match(Option.all([part.number, number]), {
    onSome: ([numeral, next]) => [`Part ${numeral}, Chapter ${next}: ${title}`],
    onNone: () => [
      ...Option.toArray(Option.map(part.name, (name) => `Part: ${name}`)),
      `Chapter: ${title}`,
    ],
  })

const openingOf = (book: Book, ref: BoxRef): Opening => {
  const own = chapterOf(book, ref.module)
  const part = Option.match(own, { onNone: () => noPart, onSome: (chapter) => chapter.part })
  const number = nextNumber(book, part)
  const title = ref.name.value
  const body = textOf(ref.body)
  const language = Option.flatMap(own, (chapter) =>
    Option.fromNullishOr(chapter.module.doc.frontmatter?.language),
  )
  const numbered = Option.match(number, {
    onNone: () => '',
    onSome: (next) => `${String(next).padStart(2, '0')}-`,
  })
  return {
    file: `${folderOf(ref.module)}${numbered}${slugOf(title)}.loom`,
    text: Array.join(
      [
        '---',
        ...membership(part, number, title),
        'Status: Canvas',
        ...Option.toArray(Option.map(language, (written) => `Language: ${written.value}`)),
        '---',
        '',
        `# ${title}`,
        '',
        ...(body === '' ? [] : [body, '']),
      ],
      '\n',
    ),
  }
}

~

`boxNoticesIn` tells the diagnostics what each chapter box of one loom needs, as a `BoxNotice`. A box whose chapter is not written is `Unwritten`, with the opening that writes it. A box whose body no longer matches its chapter's summary is `Stale`, with the chapter's title, the `summary` the body should hold, and the span of the body, from the line after the opener to the start of the closer. A box that never closes has no body to compare, so it earns no notice, beside the fault the document builder already gives it.

`refreshedSummaries` brings every stale box of the corpus back in step, the way `loom refresh` does. It rewrites each loom that holds a stale box, replacing each body with its chapter's summary, last box first, so each replacement leaves the spans before it where they were.

=>

export type BoxNotice = Data.TaggedEnum<{
  Unwritten: { readonly name: BoxNameToken; readonly opening: Opening }
  Stale: {
    readonly name: BoxNameToken
    readonly chapter: string
    readonly summary: string
    readonly body: Position
  }
}>

export const BoxNotice = Data.taggedEnum<BoxNotice>()

type StaleBox = Data.TaggedEnum.Value<BoxNotice, 'Stale'>

const bodySpan = (ref: BoxRef, closer: LoomWeft): Position => ({
  start: { line: ref.opener.position.start.line + 1, offset: ref.opener.position.end.offset },
  end: closer.position.start,
})

const noticeOf = (book: Book, ref: BoxRef): Option.Option<BoxNotice> =>
  Option.match(boxChapter(book, ref), {
    onNone: () =>
      Option.some(BoxNotice.Unwritten({ name: ref.name, opening: openingOf(book, ref) })),
    onSome: (chapter) => {
      const summary = openingProseOf(chapter.module)
      return pipe(
        ref.closer,
        Option.filter(() => textOf(ref.body) !== summary),
        Option.map((closer) =>
          BoxNotice.Stale({
            name: ref.name,
            chapter: chapter.title,
            summary: summary === '' ? '' : `${summary}\n`,
            body: bodySpan(ref, closer),
          }),
        ),
      )
    },
  })

const noticesOf = (book: Book, module: Location): ReadonlyArray<BoxNotice> =>
  Array.getSomes(Array.map(boxesIn(book, module), (ref) => noticeOf(book, ref)))

export const boxNoticesIn = (corpus: LoomCorpusAst, module: LoomModule): ReadonlyArray<BoxNotice> =>
  noticesOf(bookOf(corpus.modules), module.path)

const withSummaries = (text: string, stale: ReadonlyArray<StaleBox>): string =>
  Array.reduce(
    Array.sort(
      stale,
      Order.mapInput(Order.Number, (box: StaleBox) => -box.body.start.offset),
    ),
    text,
    (written, box) =>
      written.slice(0, box.body.start.offset) + box.summary + written.slice(box.body.end.offset),
  )

export const refreshedSummaries = (
  corpus: LoomCorpusAst,
): ReadonlyArray<{ readonly path: Location; readonly text: string }> => {
  const book = bookOf(corpus.modules)
  return pipe(
    Array.fromIterable(corpus.modules.values()),
    Array.map((module) => ({
      module,
      stale: Array.filter(noticesOf(book, module.path), BoxNotice.$is('Stale')),
    })),
    Array.filter(({ stale }) => stale.length > 0),
    Array.map(({ module, stale }) => ({
      path: module.path,
      text: withSummaries(module.text, stale),
    })),
  )
}

## Diagnostics across the corpus

Every node carries a health, and a health holds diagnostics. `diagnosticsIn` is the walk that reads them out. It descends any node, gathers the diagnostics it carries, and skips the `health` field itself, so a node's diagnostics are collected once rather than again at every child. It keeps every severity — error, warning, and informational note alike — because the editor surfaces them all.

`moduleDiagnostics` is the per-file view: one module's diagnostics from both tiers, the grammatical health on its parsed document and the semantic health on its product. The product pass lands each fault it can judge within one loom on the part it builds for that anchor. An ambiguous anchor leaves an empty `Fragment` carrying the fault, and a cross-language anchor leaves a `NameRef` carrying it. An edge that leaves the loom carries no fault here, because the compiler judges it against the whole corpus. So the walk reads the `product.code` alone. A tangle sink's `File` repeats that same `Code`, and reading it too would count every sink's diagnostics twice.

An anchor that reaches beyond its loom is the one anchor no single module can judge. Its edge names a section the product pass never saw, so the compiler judges it against the whole corpus. The compiler adds what it finds to the module's own diagnostics, both for the open `.loom` in the editor and for the tangle.

A chapter's title is no single module's to judge either, because it is a name among the chapters of its part. `namesakesOf` finds the other looms whose chapters share a module's title within its part, with the title they share, and the compiler raises the `DuplicateName` fault on that title. Two chapters stand in one part when `samePart` says their parts are one. A chapter that names no part has no siblings to clash with. Chapters with one title in two parts are no clash either, since a path through the part tells them apart.

`corpusErrors` is the whole-corpus view the tangler takes, narrowed to errors. It reads each module's diagnostics through `diagnosticsOf`, keeps the error-severity ones, and returns every failing module with them. So the tangler refuses a broken build with one report rather than failing at the first bad file. `diagnosticsOf` defaults to `moduleDiagnostics`, and the compiler passes a reader that adds the cross-file judgement. A warning never blocks a tangle; only an error does.

=>

const diagnosticsIn = (node: unknown): ReadonlyArray<Diagnostic> => {
  if (Array.isArray(node)) return node.flatMap(diagnosticsIn)
  if (node === null || typeof node !== 'object') return []
  const self =
    'health' in node
      ? (node as { health: { diagnostics: ReadonlyArray<Diagnostic> } }).health.diagnostics
      : []
  const nested = Object.entries(node).flatMap(([key, value]) =>
    key === 'health' ? [] : diagnosticsIn(value),
  )
  return [...self, ...nested]
}

export const moduleDiagnostics = (module: LoomModule): ReadonlyArray<Diagnostic> => [
  ...diagnosticsIn(module.doc),
  ...diagnosticsIn(module.product.code),
]

export const corpusErrors = (
  corpus: LoomCorpusAst,
  diagnosticsOf: (module: LoomModule) => ReadonlyArray<Diagnostic> = moduleDiagnostics,
): ReadonlyArray<{
  readonly path: Location
  readonly diagnostics: ReadonlyArray<Diagnostic>
}> =>
  pipe(
    Array.fromIterable(corpus.modules.values()),
    Array.map((m) => {
      const diagnostics = Array.filter(diagnosticsOf(m), (d) => d.severity === 'error')
      return diagnostics.length === 0 ? Option.none() : Option.some({ path: m.path, diagnostics })
    }),
    Array.getSomes,
  )

export type Namesakes = {
  readonly title: { readonly source: string; readonly position: Position }
  readonly looms: ReadonlyArray<Location>
}

export const namesakesOf = (book: Book, module: LoomModule): Option.Option<Namesakes> =>
  pipe(
    Option.all({ chapter: chapterOf(book, module.path), title: chapterTitleToken(module) }),
    Option.flatMap(({ chapter, title }) =>
      pipe(
        book.chapters,
        Array.filter(
          (other) =>
            other.module.path !== module.path &&
            other.title === chapter.title &&
            samePart(chapter.part, other.part),
        ),
        Array.map((other) => other.module.path),
        Option.liftPredicate(Array.isReadonlyArrayNonEmpty),
        Option.map((looms) => ({ title, looms })),
      ),
    ),
  )

## What this module draws on

Effect supplies `Schema` for the model, `Array`, `Option`, and `pipe` for the queries, `Data` for the places a lookup walks through, and `Match` for the dispatch on a place and on the kind under a cursor. The module bundles the parse model `LoomDocument` and the product model `ProductSchema`, with `LoomSection`, the unit the corpus walks, the `WarpAnchorToken` an anchor is, the `WarpToken` a value warp is defined by, the `TocWeft` a contents line is — with the `TocTitleToken` it names a chapter and the `TocPartToken` it names a part — and `Diagnostic`, `Position`, and `Point`, what a corpus walk gathers, where a diagnostic lands, and where a segment of an anchor's name begins and ends. `symbolAt` and `profileOf` from `LoomSymbol` name the token under a cursor and read its features, so the navigation reads the same table the mappings and colours do. `boxOf`, `boxKind`, and `chapterKind` find the chapter boxes of a loom, and `Order` sorts the stale ones from last to first.

=>

import { Array, Data, Match, Option, Order, pipe, Schema } from 'effect'
import { type Diagnostic, type Point, type Position } from '#ast/LoomNode'
import {
  boxKind,
  type BoxNameToken,
  type BoxToken,
  chapterKind,
  type HeadingTitleToken,
  type TocPartToken,
  type TocTitleToken,
  type WarpAnchorToken,
  type WarpToken,
} from '#ast/LoomTokens'
import { boxOf, type LoomWeft, type TocWeft } from '#ast/Weft'
import { ProductSchema } from '#ast/ProductAst'
import { LoomDocumentSchema, type LoomDocument, type LoomSection } from '#ast/LoomAst'
import { profileOf, symbolAt } from '#ast/LoomSymbol'

